@Graphics editing
This menu can be used to edit the graphics for the game. If the graphics
are not defined then the default graphics (PC characters and colours) will
be used, so using this menu is not mandatory for making a game world.

!chr;Font (simple)
!fnt;Font (advanced)
!pal;Palette
!rop;Backdrop

!glo;Global options

:chr;Font (simple)
This is for making simple 8x14 full fonts with 256 characters. These fonts
cannot include animations, character code mapping, etc, and cannot be
partial fonts, and are also not compressed.

:fnt;Font (advanced)
Not implemented yet.

:pal;Palette
Defines a palette. There are three kind of palettes, which are according to
the number of colours: 16, 64, or 128.

The usual number of colours for a palette is sixteen, which is used in the
usual text-only video mode; each character cell has one of these sixteen
colours for the foreground and another for the background.

Palettes with 64 or 128 colours are used for SMZX mode and graphical mode,
and are not meaningful otherwise.

When you create a new palette, you can also import it from a file. See the
fileformat.doc format for a description of the expected file format.

:rop;Backdrop
A backdrop is a 640x350 indexed colour picture, which can also include
Z-index data. In graphical mode, you can optionally display a backdrop
instead of the text layer.

Backdrops cannot be created using the Super ZZ Zero editor; you must use an
external program. However, you can import and preview backdrops.

Backdrops may specify palettes to be loaded when loading the backdrop, so
that it will be displayed with the correct colours. If the palettes are not
specified inside of the backdrop file, then they must be loaded by the use
of the #LOADPAL command or the variable property list.

:glo;Global options
The graphics global options contains the following settings:

þ 40 columns: If selected, then the display during the game will use 40
columns; if not selected, then it will use 80 columns. Some built-in menus
will always use 80 columns regardless of this. Note that if it is set to 40
columns then only the first 40 columns of the screen are used and the other
40 columns will not be displayed.

þ SMZX: If selected, then the display during the game uses SMZX mode. See
the below section about SMZX mode.
!smzx;SMZX mode

þ Mandatory font/palettes: If selected, then it means that this world file
requires these fonts/palettes for the game to work properly. Super ZZ Zero
will refuse to run the world if you try to run it on a computer that is not
capable of using these features.

þ Recommended font/palettes: If selected, then it is recommended to use the
custom fonts/palettes included in the world file; it will not work as well,
but is still playable, if they are not used. A warning message will be
displayed if you try to run it on a computer that cannot use custom fonts.

þ Main font: The name of the main font to use. Leave it blank to use the
built-in PC font.

þ Main palette: The name of the main palette to use. Leave it blank to use
the built-in PC colours.

:smzx;SMZX mode
In SMZX mode, the horizontal resolution per character is half (resulting in
4x14) if it is 80 columns screen mode, or the vertical resolution per
character is half (resulting in 8x7) if it is 40 columns screen mode. These
will double the pixels, so that it uses two pixels on the screen for one
character pixel in this way, although you can now display four colours per
character instead of only two colours, so the number of bits is still the
same as it is normally.

Two adjacent pixels in a character raster are effectively combined into a
single pixel. These are assigned four colour numbers, from I to IV,
according to which of the individual bits are set or clear.

There are two kind of SMZX modes, which can be used on the same screen at
the same time.

Normal SMZX mode uses a palette with 128 colours. Colour I uses the
background colour (the high nybble of the tile colour) as both the high
and low nybbles of the colour to select from the palette (except that the
high bit is ignored, since the palette has only 7-bit colours). Colour II
uses the foreground as the high nybble, colour III uses the foreground as
the low nybble, and colour IV uses the foreground as both nybbles.

Alternate SMZX mode uses a palette with 64 colours. The high 2-bits of the
palette index are according to the bits of the character raster, and of
those two bits, the lower one selects which nybble of the tile colour will
be used as the low 4-bits of the palette index.

SMZX mode cannot be combined with graphical mode, although the colours that
are usable in SMZX mode can also be used in graphical mode.
